    description: "ADR-0023 §5 reserves the *publish family* for a per-family audit before producer-side migration (Phase 3). Currently event_publishes is in the registry at pending_classification; the rest of the family (event_subscribes, crdt_publishes, annotated_publishes) is emitted by analyzers but absent from the registry, surfaced by the comprehensive sweep at PR #3464 close-out. This item is the audit.\n\nCONTEXT (for a dev picking this up cold):\n\nADR-0023 (docs/adr/0023-edge-type-relationship-not-endpoints.md, currently Draft) classifies edge_type values on the relationship / endpoint_shape / pending_classification axis. ADR-0023 §5 explicitly defers the dispatch and publish families to per-family audits because the family members are NOT obviously a single relationship under different protocols — some may be genuinely distinct, others protocol-conditional aliases. From ADR-0023 §5 (transcribed):\n\n> **Publish family:** event_publishes, crdt_publishes, annotated_publishes,\n> ...\n> ... per-family audit during migration: some may be genuinely distinct\n> relationships, others are protocol-conditional labels.\n\nPUBLISH-FAMILY MEMBERS (registered values currently at pending_classification, plus emit-site values surfaced by WI-tavas-voror's sweep that likely belong here):\n\n- event_publishes (registry, pending_classification) — emit an event to an event bus\n- event_subscribes (sweep, unregistered) — register as a listener for events; arguably the inverse direction of publishes (or a separate axis-bearing relationship altogether)\n- crdt_publishes (sweep, unregistered) — publish to a CRDT-backed event log; framework-specific publish variant\n- annotated_publishes (sweep, unregistered) — publish driven by annotations / decorators; framework-specific publish variant\n- emits (sweep, unregistered) — generic 'emit' relationship, possibly broader than publish; audit should determine whether it's publish-family or generic-relationship-shaped\n- enqueues (sweep, unregistered) — push to a queue; possibly publish-family with queue as protocol\n\nTHE AUDIT QUESTIONS (per ADR-0023's Decision):\n\nFor each family member, decide:\n\n1. CANONICAL — names a genuinely distinct relationship. Promote to the relationship axis with a clear axiom-conformant description (the value names the relationship the edge expresses between src and dst). Likely candidates: event_publishes (the publish action), event_subscribes (the subscribe action — but verify it is not really a 'references the event channel' relationship). Direction matters — publishes and subscribes are converse relationships if we treat them as edges between code and channel; if we treat them as edges between code and OTHER code via the channel, they may collapse.\n\n2. FOLD — protocol-conditional or framework-specific alias of an existing canonical. Deprecate the family-specific name; rename to canonical (likely event_publishes or a generic 'emits') plus edge.meta carrying the framework / protocol / channel-kind. Likely candidates: crdt_publishes (CRDT is a channel kind, not a separate relationship), annotated_publishes (annotation is a syntactic mechanism, not a separate relationship).\n\n3. DEPRECATE-NO-FOLD — the value's meaning is not actually a relationship at all. Document migration to a different field (Symbol property, edge.meta key, etc.) if applicable.\n\nApply the four leakage tests from the audit playbook (Step 3 — property derivability, apex/peer overloading, construct vs. relationship, mechanism vs. category). Each verdict requires a written rationale (what query each value answers; why it does or does not collapse with siblings; whether the channel-kind / framework is endpoint-shape leakage that should live in src/dst.kind or in edge.meta).\n\nDESIGN QUESTIONS to surface during the audit:\n\n- Is 'subscribes' actually the converse of 'publishes', or are both pointing FROM code TO a channel/topic Symbol? If the latter, both are 'references' relationships with meta['role'] = 'publisher' / 'subscriber' — the family collapses dramatically.\n- Is 'emits' broader than 'publish'? E.g., a function that emits a side effect (logging, metrics) — not pub-sub but the same verb in code. The audit may need to split 'emits' into multiple canonicals.\n- 'enqueues' — is it publish-family (push to a message queue) or its own family (queue is a different topology from event-bus pub-sub)?\n\nDELIVERABLES:\n\n1. Per-value verdict table in this item's discussion thread (audit-style — one row per family member).\n2. ADR amendment or per-family ADR-addendum capturing the verdicts (sibling pattern: ADR-0024 for axis declarations; this is the publish-family analogue).\n3. Registry updates: pending_classification → relationship (for canonicals), endpoint_shape (for fold-deprecations), or removal (for deprecate-no-fold).\n4. Update WI-tavas-voror's discussion thread to reflect any sweep-surfaced family members that the audit resolved.\n\nDEPENDENCIES (HARD):\n- ADR-0023 advancement to Proposed/Accepted is desirable so the §5 framing is settled before audits commit verdicts to the registry.\n\nDEPENDENCIES (SOFT):\n- WI-tavas-voror-rakiv-litog-gatad-zonoz-holah-tugil — overlaps on the sweep-surfaced family members. The audit consumes the sweep's findings; coordinate so each value is classified once.\n- Phase 3 (WI-mokam-jalig-fuhab-gifug-sigij-gotar-sisig-zufoz) — the publish-family producer sites cannot be migrated until this audit picks canonical names for the renames.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n- Every publish-family member listed above has a documented verdict (canonical / fold / deprecate) with rationale.\n- The registry reflects the verdicts.\n- An ADR amendment or addendum captures the verdicts as durable governance.\n- The next sweep (e.g., Phase 3 producer migration starting on this family) inherits the canonical landings.\n\nSIBLINGS:\n- Dispatch-family audit (sibling: WI-kofuh-fovar-mazak-dogar-kaluj-sapod-rumog-porol) — same audit shape, different value set, different framework expertise (pub-sub vs. routing/dispatch).\n- WI-sahab-fatoz, WI-mokam-jalig, WI-vomoj-suhaz — Phase 2/3/4. Phase 3's publish-family-touching subset waits on this audit.\n- WI-tavas-voror — 45-value triage. This audit resolves the publish-family subset (~5 values) of those 45.\n\nBACKGROUND: surfaced as part of loose-end #9 in the audit-lifecycle session (Apr 2026). ADR-0023 §5 explicitly deferred these family audits; tracker captures the work."  # d29f
    fields: {}  # d29f
